Common DevOps Pipeline Errors & How I Fixed Them (Real-Time Scenarios)🚀

Today I wanted to share a few common DevOps CI/CD pipeline issues I’ve faced in real projects—and the exact resolutions that helped me fix them quickly.

Hope this helps someone debugging their pipeline today! 

1. “Git Checkout Failed / Invalid Credentials”
Cause: Wrong Git credentials, expired PAT token, or incorrect repo permissions.
Fix:

Regenerated a new Git Personal Access Token (PAT)

Updated credentials in Jenkins/AWS CodePipeline/GitHub Actions

Ensured repo-level read permissions

2. “Maven Build Failure – Dependencies Not Found”
Cause: Missing dependencies in pom.xml or repository not reachable.
Fix:

Cleaned workspace → mvn clean install

Checked Nexus/Artifactory availability

Fixed incorrect version in pom.xml

3. “Docker Build Fails – No Space Left on Device”
Cause: Old images/containers taking up disk.
Fix:

docker system prune -a
docker volume prune
Increased disk size on the build agent

 4. “Helm Deployment Error – Kubernetes API Timeout”
Cause: Cluster connectivity issue or kubeconfig mismatch.
 Fix:

Re-authenticated cluster

Cleared old kubeconfig context

Verified API server availability

Checked if Ingress, Services, and Pods are up

5.“Pipeline Fails Due to Hardcoded Paths / Missing Files”
 Cause: Developers using absolute paths like /home/user/...
Fix:

Updated pipeline to use relative paths

Added validations for missing files

Standardized build directory structure

6. “Jenkins Not Triggering Automatically”
 Cause: Misconfigured Git Webhook.
Fix:

Re-configured webhook with correct Jenkins URL

Opened firewall/NAT inbound port for webhook

Enabled Push, Pull Request events

7. “Resource Quota Exceeded in Kubernetes”
Cause: Pods requesting more CPU/memory than allowed.
Fix:

Optimized resource requests/limits

Cleaned unused deployments & pods

Increased namespace quota when required

Key Learning
DevOps pipelines fail all the time — what matters is how quickly and confidently you can identify the root cause, apply the right fix, and prevent it from happening again.
